  "body": "What happens: `examples/first-4axis-part` (from #2135) cuts four flats that are all 0.360 in from the A centreline over the same X span (-0.75 to -0.25 in, `examples/models/gen_first_4axis_part_obj.py`). After each `G0 A-90 / A0 / A90 / A180` the posted XYZ moves are therefore identical. That is correct for this part, but a reader cannot see that each indexed op is planned in its own rotated plane - the maintainer read it as \"it never relocates the plane with the A changes\".\n\nThe relocation itself is pinned elsewhere: `crates/app/tests/fourth_axis_pose.rs` (a flat under the bar, stated in world coordinates, posts as A180 on the skin) and the tilted-pocket tests in `crates/app/tests/area_controls.rs`.\n\nExpected: the example shows the relocation in its own program.\n\nProposal: give the flats different depths and X spans per side (for example A0 at 0.360 in over X -0.75..-0.25, A90 at 0.350 in over X -1.00..-0.60, A180 at 0.340 in over X -0.50..-0.20, A-90 at 0.355 in over X -0.90..-0.40), regenerate the model, project and `.nc`, and update the guide's G-code excerpt. Keep the part inside the 770MX + microARC 4 envelope and the 1/4 in tool's reach.\n\nDone means:\n- each `G0 A..` block in `examples/first-4axis-part.nc` posts its own Z depth and X span;\n- `crates/app/tests/first_4axis_part.rs` pins, per side, the posted depth and span against the model's numbers;\n- the guide page \"Your first 4-axis part\" shows and explains the per-side difference;\n- verify stays clean (no gouge) for every side.\n",
